{"name":"Deep Ocean Floor","hex":"#2c4173","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"floor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["floor function"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The interior bottom or surface of a house or building; the supporting surface of a room.","The bottom surface of a natural structure, entity, or space (e.g. cave, forest, ocean, desert, etc.); the ground (surface of the Earth)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/floor"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seabed","blocks":[{"text":"The seabed is the bottom of the ocean. Alternatively, it is known as the seafloor, sea floor, ocean floor, or ocean bottom. The floor of all the world's oceans is known as the seabed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Common_stingray_tenerife.jpg/330px-Common_stingray_tenerife.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seabed"},{"title":"Deep sea","blocks":[{"text":"The deep sea is broadly defined as the ocean depth where light begins to fade, at an approximate depth of 200 m (660 ft) or the point of transition from continental shelves to continental slopes. Conditions within the deep sea are a combination of low temperatures, darkness, and high pressure. The deep sea is considered the least explored Earth biome as the extreme conditions make the environment difficult to access and explore."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Schematic_representation_of_pelagic_and_benthic_zones.jpg/330px-Schematic_representation_of_pelagic_and_benthic_zones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_sea"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harbouring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean"},{"title":"Floor","blocks":[{"text":"A floor is the bottom surface of typically an enclosed space such as a room or vehicle. Floors vary from simple dirt in a cave to many layered surfaces made with modern technology. Floors may be stone, wood, bamboo, metal, or any other material that can support the expected load."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Parquet_flooring_in_Mus%C3%A9e_des_arts_d%C3%A9coratifs_de_Strasbourg.jpg/330px-Parquet_flooring_in_Mus%C3%A9e_des_arts_d%C3%A9coratifs_de_Strasbourg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floor"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Bathymetry of the ocean floor showing the continental shelves and oceanic plateaus (red), the mid-ocean ridges (yellow-green) and the abyssal plains (blue to purple). Like land terrain, the ocean floor has mountains including volcanoes, ridges, valleys, and plains.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMid-ocean_ridge_system.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Il_fondale_di_Giove.jpg/330px-Il_fondale_di_Giove.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Il_fondale_di_Giove.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Il_fondale_di_Giove.jpg/330px-Il_fondale_di_Giove.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIl_fondale_di_Giove.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Stingray_%28Myliobatoidei%29_in_Cozumel%2C_Mexico.jpg/500px-Stingray_%28Myliobatoidei%29_in_Cozumel%2C_Mexico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Sti